tara:TARA_1840369 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Yuan-Primary Point" ;
    dc:description "Each of the twelve main meridians has a Yuan-Primary Point, which is located on the limbs. The Chinese character (Yuan) means primary qi in this context. The chapter \"The 66th Medical Problem\" in Classic of Medical Problems describes the relation between the Yuan-Primary Points and Yuan-Primary Qi. The Yuan-Primary Qi, originating below the umbilicus and between the kidneys, is dispersed to the zang-fu organs and further to the limbs via Triple Energizer. The sites where the Yuan-Primary Qi is retained are Yuan-Primary Points, which are used to treat disorders of the zang-fu organs. In the yin meridians, the Yuan-Primary Poims overlap with the Shu-Stream Points of the Five Shu Points. Each yang meridian, however, has its Yuan-Primary Point other than the Shu-Stream Point." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    tara:hasSpecialPointRole tara:TARA_3455429 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( tara:TARA_4943316 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom tara:TARA_3455429 ] ) ] .

tara:TARA_2954517 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Jing-Well Point" ;
    dc:description "One of the five Shu Points, the Jing-Well point is situated in the place where the meridian qi starts to bubble." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    tara:hasSpecialPointRole tara:TARA_3839815 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( tara:TARA_1787498 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom tara:TARA_3839815 ] ) ] .

tara:TARA_3861175 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Back-Shu Point" ;
    dc:description "Back-Shu points are specific points on the back where the qi of the respective zang-fu organs is infused. It is stated in Chapter 51 of Miraculous Pivot that \"in the Back-Shu Points you are looking for the reactionary spots of tenderness and soreness, or the points on which pressure exerted relieves pain and discomforts of the patient\". Situated close to their respectively related zang-fu organs, the Back-Shu points present abnormal reactions to the dysfunction of their corresponding zang-fu organs. They are often used for disorders of the internal organs." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    tara:hasSpecialPointRole tara:TARA_9507527 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( tara:TARA_2614885 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom tara:TARA_9507527 ] ) ] .

tara:TARA_6200120 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Main Meridian" ;
    IAO:0000115 "One of the twelve Meridians that is directly affiliated with a zang or fu organ system via hasAssociatedOrgan, and that is linked to the other eleven principal meridians in a fixed circulatory sequence." ;
    dc:description """As the major part in the meridian system, the twelve main meridians share the following features. Each with its own acupuncture points is distributed on a fixed portion of the body surface; each pertains to either a zang or a fu organ (those that pertain to the zang organ communicating with the fu organ, and vice versa). 

The twelve main meridians link one another in a fixed order. A cyclical now of qi is maintained by the connection of the meridians of the hand and foot, yin and yang, exterior and interior.""" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Page 65. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    tara:hasSynonym "Primary meridian" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tara:TARA_7226516 .

tara:TARA_6705086 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Shu-Stream Point" ;
    dc:description "One of the five Shu Points, the Shu-Stream point is where the meridian qi flourishes. In the yin meridians, the Yuan-Primary Points overlap with the Shu-Stream points of the five Shu Points. Each yang meridian, however, has its Yuan-Primary Point other than the Shu-Stream Point." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    tara:hasSpecialPointRole tara:TARA_1716565 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( tara:TARA_1787498 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom tara:TARA_1716565 ] ) ] .

tara:TARA_7315457 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Jing-River Point" ;
    dc:description "One of the five Shu Points, the Jing-River point is where the meridian qi is pouring abundantly." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    tara:hasSpecialPointRole tara:TARA_1758929 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( tara:TARA_1787498 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom tara:TARA_1758929 ] ) ] .

tara:TARA_8746709 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Front-Mu Point" ;
    dc:description "Front-Mu points are those points on the chest and abdomen where the qi of the respective zang-fu organs is infused and converged. Located close to their corresponding zang-fu organs, the Front-Mu points play a significant role in the diagnosis and treatment of the disorders of the internal organs." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    tara:hasSpecialPointRole tara:TARA_7571236 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( tara:TARA_2614885 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom tara:TARA_7571236 ] ) ] .

tara:TARA_9857829 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Ying-Spring Point" ;
    dc:description "One of the five Shu Points, the Ying-Spring point is where the meridian qi starts to gush." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    tara:hasSpecialPointRole tara:TARA_2478120 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( tara:TARA_1787498 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom tara:TARA_2478120 ] ) ] .

tara:TARA_6594221 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "He-Sea Point" ;
    dc:description "One of the five Shu Points, the He-Sea point signifies the confluence of rivers in the sea, where the meridian qi is the most flourishing. In addition, each of the six fu organs has another He-Sea point in the three yang meridians of the foot, known as the Lower He-Sea point." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    tara:hasSpecialPointRole tara:TARA_4109407 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( tara:TARA_1787498 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom tara:TARA_4109407 ] ) ] .

